# .release-removals — deliberate deletions against the PUBLISHED tree.
#
# One path or path-prefix per line; `#` comments allowed. The `[6/6] public-regress`
# gate blocks any release that removes a file already on public `master` unless it is
# declared here. A deletion is not forbidden — it must be intended, and this file is
# the reviewed record that it was. Read by dev-pipeline/promote/promote.sh.
#
# A comment-only or blank file is NOT an allow-all; it declares nothing.

# ── [ADR-0031], 2026-08-23 — the DATED status snapshots are not a publication surface ──
#
# READ THIS BEFORE READING THE PATH BELOW: `docs/status/STATUS.md` is not being
# withdrawn from the public surface. It MOVED to `docs/STATUS.md` and is declared in
# CANONICAL-DOCS.toml at its new path, in the same commit as this line. Against the
# published tree a move is a deletion plus an addition, and `public-regress` only sees
# the deletion half — so it has to be declared here, and the declaration would otherwise
# read as "we took the status doc down," which is the opposite of what happened.
#
# What IS withdrawn: everything else under `docs/status/` — the dated
# STATUS-*/HANDOFF-*/CHECKPOINT-*/SESSION-* snapshots. Those are working memory: dated
# measurements, seat names, blockers, half-verified numbers. The ecosystem pin
# measurement found 466 of 670 unreachable commit citations fleet-wide living in exactly
# these files, and sweeping them is the wrong fix because they regenerate the problem by
# being used as intended. (None of the 466 are ours — this repo and entity-core-protocol
# both measure zero, which is why [ADR-0031]'s first draft, which banned the directory
# and took the rolling log with it, was corrected the same day.)
#
# Why a PREFIX and not the one file: the separation now runs by path rather than by
# filename, so `docs/status/` can be dropped wholesale and the canonical log is never in
# the blast radius. That is the durable half of the fix.
#
# What a reader loses: nothing they came for. README.md answers "what is this",
# CHANGELOG.md "what changed", CONFORMANCE-MATRIX.md "is it conformant and against
# what", docs/STATUS.md "where does it stand" — all four declared canonical and written
# for that reader.
docs/status

# ── 2026-09 — the origination axis is retired: 31 `run-origination-core.sh` ──────
#
# WHAT A CALLER LOSES: the command. `protocol-generator/<lang>/run-origination-core.sh`
# is gone from all 31 peers that had one, with no shim and no replacement at that path.
# If you invoke it from CI, a wrapper, or habit, it will not be found.
#
# WHAT A CALLER DOES NOT LOSE: the coverage. The axis carried three checks — the §6.11
# dispatch-outbound-reentry probes against the reference peer — and they were only ever
# run for the 31 peers that happened to have a harness. They now run for ALL 46 peers
# inside the ordinary conformance census, `protocol-generator/<lang>/run-s4.sh`, which
# every caller already runs. That is why the axis was retired rather than deprecated:
# a second entry point for checks the main one now performs is a second answer to the
# same question, and the two can drift.
#
# ⚠ THE READER-FACING RESIDUE, STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED. Deleting the script did not
# delete the sentences that name it. 24 peer `README.md` files still print it as a
# runnable command, and the per-peer `status/` reports cite it in their measurement
# records. The reports are dated evidence of what was run on a day and are correct as
# written; the READMEs are wrong and are being corrected as a separate pass, because a
# 119-file mechanical rewrite landing inside a release cut is the shape that has already
# damaged pinned files in this tree once. **If a README tells you to run it, the command
# you want is that peer's `run-s4.sh`.**
#
# ENUMERATED, NOT DECLARED BY PREFIX — and this is the opposite call to the one below.
# There is no directory here that was retired; the 31 files sit one per language tree
# beside everything those trees still publish, so the only prefix that would cover them
# is `protocol-generator` itself — every peer, every corpus, every source file. That is
# not a declaration, it is an exemption. The list is also CLOSED: the axis is gone, so
# no future peer can add a line to it. A list that is complete and cannot grow is worth
# writing out; a prefix that covers a product directory forever is not.
