# ── 2026-09 — the test-vector corpora are de-versioned ───────────────────────────
#
# `protocol-generator/shared/test-vectors/v0.8.0/` is gone, all nine files with it.
# A corpus is identified by its NAME and its own `CHANGELOG.md`, never by a
# spec-revision stamp in its path or an artifact version in its filename — a stamped
# path claims a correspondence to a spec revision that nothing maintains. The nine are
# replaced by name-identified corpora:
#
#   v0.8.0/conformance-vectors-v1.{cbor,diag}  →  ecf-conformance/conformance-vectors.{cbor,diag}
#   v0.8.0/agility-vectors-v1.{cbor,diag}      →  crypto-agility/agility-vectors.{cbor,diag}
#   v0.8.0/agility-SEEDS.md                    →  crypto-agility/SEEDS.md
#   v0.8.0/type-registry-vectors-v1.{cbor,diag},  →  type-registry/…
#     type-registry-shapes.json
#   v0.8.0/MANIFEST.md                         →  shared/test-vectors/README.md, which
#                                                 records the vendor pins and every
#                                                 RETIRED digest
#
# ⚠ IF YOU PINNED THE OLD AGILITY PATH, YOU PINNED RETRACTED BYTES, AND THIS IS NOT A
# RENAME. `v0.8.0/agility-vectors-v1.cbor` was a second, SUPERSEDED copy of the
# crypto-agility corpus — the correctly-vendored one was already published beside it
# under `crypto-agility/`, and the two did not agree. The old copy pinned a re-hash
# construction that the current specification requires a peer to REFUSE, and one case
# asserted the wrong content hash for the same reason. Nothing failed while both copies
# existed, because every consumer reading the old path got the old bytes and agreed with
# them. Four peers were measured across the change and they split by how they consume the
# corpus, not by language: the two that LOAD it began failing the moment the duplicate
# went — real defects in those peers, now fixed — and the two that TRANSCRIBE it had been
# passing while carrying the identical defect, because peer and test were wrong in the
# same direction. Repointing at `crypto-agility/` is therefore a correctness fix, not a
# path change.
#
# A PREFIX, and here it is the right shape: the directory itself is what was retired,
# and a directory cannot lose a file to this without losing all of them.
protocol-generator/shared/test-vectors/v0.8.0

# ── 2026-09 — the Dart prefetch layer no longer carries its own dependency pins ──
#
# `containers/dart-toolchain/prefetch/` held a second, throwaway `pubspec.yaml` that
# pinned the peer's three direct dependencies exactly and let every transitive
# dependency float. AN EXACT PIN ON THE DIRECT DEPENDENCIES IS NOT A PIN ON WHAT LANDS
# IN THE CACHE: on the next image rebuild the transitives re-resolved upstream and the
# cache drifted out from under a lockfile that had not moved, so the peer would not
# start at all. The prefetch now seeds from the Dart peer's OWN `pubspec.lock`, so the
# cache is that lockfile's closure by construction and the two cannot drift; a lockfile
# that stops resolving now fails the image build, which is the right place to find out.
# The second copy of the pins is deleted rather than re-synced — two copies of a pin set
# is the defect, not the fix. Nothing outside the build image read this file.
containers/dart-toolchain/prefetch
